Chain of Custody
All evidence was hashed at the time of acquisition and stored in an isolated forensic directory to ensure integrity and prevent tampering.

Methodology

The analysis was performed using standard forensic tools:

- Autopsy → disk and file system analysis  
- Volatility 3 → memory analysis and process inspection  
- Wireshark → network traffic analysis  

### Why These Tools Were Used
Each tool covers a different layer of system behavior:
- Disk tools show stored and deleted files  
- Memory tools reveal active malware behavior  
- Network tools show external communication  

No modifications were made to original evidence files.

Detailed Findings

Disk Analysis
- Found suspicious executable: `update.exe`  
- Recovered deleted PowerShell script from Recycle Bin  
- Evidence of user execution activity present in file system artifacts  

Memory Analysis
- Active process: `update.exe`  
- Parent process: `explorer.exe`  
- External connection to IP: `185.222.202.11`  
- Evidence of hidden execution within system memory  

Network Analysis
- Repeated HTTP POST requests detected  
- Encoded outbound data observed  
- Consistent communication with a single external IP  

This behavior is consistent with command-and-control (C2) activity.

Timeline Reconstruction

Time Event 
14:32 File downloaded (`update.exe`)
14:33 User executes file 
14:33 Process appears in memory 
14:34 Network communication begins 

Conclusion and Assessment

The investigation confirms that the Windows 11 system was compromised through execution of a malicious file.

How the Compromise Occurred
The user executed `update.exe`, which initiated malware behavior including memory residency and external communication.

Evidence Supporting This Conclusion
- Disk artifacts showing file presence and execution  
- Memory evidence of active malicious process  
- Network evidence of external communication  
- Correlation across all evidence types  

Recommended Actions (Real-World Context)
- Isolate and reimage affected systems  
- Block malicious IP addresses  
- Investigate potential lateral movement  
- Monitor for repeated indicators of compromise  

Confidence Level
High confidence due to consistent evidence across multiple independent sources.

